In this lesson we'll look at anti-differentiation, which is just a fancy way of saying "reverse the derivative." If differentiating takes you from a function to its slope, anti-differentiation does the opposite and brings you back to a family of possible original functions.

Here's the big idea. If someone hands you a derivative and asks, "what could the original function have been?" you work backwards. So if the derivative is two x, the original could be x squared plus something, and that something is just a constant we call big C. Notice why C matters: both x squared plus seven and x squared minus five differentiate down to two x, so the constant is unfixable from the derivative alone. Every time you anti-differentiate, you tack on plus C to cover all the possibilities.

Now for the rules. The power rule, reversed, says you add one to the exponent, then divide by that new exponent, as long as the exponent isn't negative one. A constant by itself just becomes that constant times x, plus C again. And finally, the sum rule: you can anti-differentiate term by term and just add the pieces together.

Now let's see it in action.